  [1mThe fork:[0m whether [38;5;153m'...'[39m fits [3minside[0m [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m or gets added [3mon top[0m of it. With [38;5;153mformat("hello world", { truncate: 8 })[39m:

  - [1mEllipsis inside the budget[0m → [38;5;153m"hello..."[39m (exactly 8 chars). The output never exceeds [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m, which is what you
    need if this is feeding a fixed-width column, a table cell, or a DB field. Costs you 3 chars of real content.
  - [1mEllipsis appended after[0m → [38;5;153m"hello wo..."[39m (11 chars). You keep the full [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m of content, but the string can
    exceed the limit — so a caller who picked [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m to fit a hard constraint will overflow it.

  This one is not cheap to change later: it's observable output, so flipping it after anyone depends on it is a breaking
  change to every caller and every snapshot.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m ellipsis inside the budget. "Max length" reading as an actual maximum is the least surprising
  contract, and it's what [38;5;153mtextwrap.shorten[39m, CSS [38;5;153mtext-overflow[39m, and most UI truncators do.
